Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the disciples came to Jesus. They asked him, "Who is the greatest person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to him. And he stood the child in the midst of them.
Jesus said, "What I am about to tell you is the truth. You must change and become like little children. If you do not, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes empty of pride as is this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever welcomes a little one like this in my name welcomes me.
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me were to cause one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? If he did, it would be better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
How terrible it will be for the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Such things must happen. But how terrible it will be for those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot than to enter the eternal fire with two hands and two feet. For the eternal fire burns in hell forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The Parable of the Lost Sheep.
Look, don't despise one of these little ones. Here is what I tell you: their angels in heaven go at any time to see my Father who is in heaven.
<.>
What do you think? Suppose a man has a hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray. Will he not leave the ninety-nine sheep on the hills? Will he not go and search for the one that went astray?
What I am about to tell you is the truth. If he finds that sheep, he is happier with the one than with the ninety-nine who did not go astray.
The same applies to your Father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to perish. When one of them wrongs you,
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. And keep the matter between the two of you. If he listens to you, you have him back.
But what if he does not listen to you? Take one or two others along with you. The writing says, "Every charge must be established on the evidence of two or three witnesses." (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then say it to the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as your brother. Treat him as you would treat an unbeliever or a tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is true. What you shut up on earth will be shut up in heaven. And what you open on earth will be opened in heaven.
Again, here's what I tell you. Suppose two of you on Earth agree on something you are asking for. My Father in heaven will do it for you.
Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them." The servant who did not have compassion
Peter came to Jesus and asked him, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother if he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus said, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
When the king started doing this, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man was unable to pay. So an order was issued by his master. The man, his wife, his children, and all that he possessed must be sold to pay his debts.
The slave fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will restore everything."
The lady felt sorry for him. She forgave him what he owed and let him go.
But that slave went out and found another slave who owed him a little money. He grabbed him and started choking him. He said, "Pay back what you owe me!"
The other gentleman fell to his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded with him. "I will pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and ordered that the man be thrown into prison. And he would be held there until he could repay his debt.
The other servants saw what happened. It disturbed them greatly. They went and told their master everything that happened.
Then the master called the first servant in. He said, "You wicked servant, I forgave you all that debt because you begged me."
Haven't you shown mercy to another slave, just as I have shown mercy to you?
In anger, his master handed him over to the guards. He was to be punished until he repaid all his debts.
This is how my Father in heaven will treat each of you unless you forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus said, "Haven't you read that he who created them from the beginning made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave to his wife; and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
They are no longer two, but one. Therefore, what God has joined together, let no one separate.
They asked, "Then why did Moses command that a man write a divorce document for his wife and send her away?"
Jesus said, "Moses permitted you to divorce your wives because you were stubborn. But it was not like that from the beginning."
Here is what I say to you. Whoever divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man may not divorce his wife unless she is not faithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If that is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus replied, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who have been helped to live without marriage can accept it."
Some men are unable to have children because they were born that way. Some of them were made that way by other people. Others made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept to live this way, let them do so." Young children are brought to Jesus.
Some people brought their little children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man.
A man approached Jesus and asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to gain eternal life?"
Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered, "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, keep the commandments."
"But what about this?" the man asked. "You shall not murder, you shall not commit adultery, you shall not steal, you shall not give false testimony," Jesus declared.
Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and "love your neighbor as yourself." " (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commandments." "What else do I need to do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "What I tell you in truth. It is hard for rich people to enter the kingdom of heaven."
Again I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were truly amazed. They asked, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "For mortals, that is impossible. But for God, all things are possible."
Peter said to him, "We have left everything to follow you! What then will be our reward?"
What I am about to tell you is the truth, Jesus said to them. When all things are made new, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel.
Everyone who has left houses or families or fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more and will also inherit eternal life.
Many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who was a landowner. He went out early in the morning to hire people to work in his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also, go and work in my vineyard, and I will give you your right."
So they went. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock, he went out and found others still standing around him. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day without doing anything?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard."
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the manager of the workers. He said to him, "Call the workers and pay them their wages. Begin with the last ones you hired, and then go to the first ones."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock came. Each one of them received their usual day's wage.
When those who had been hired first came, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received the usual day's wage.
When they took it over, they started complaining about the owner.
They said: "These last people you hired only worked for one hour. You paid them the same wage that you paid us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day."
One of them answered the owner. He said, "My friend, I am being fair with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual daily wage?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give those I recently hired the same salary I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel cheated because I gave others too freely?
Those who are last will be first, and those who are first will be last. Jesus speaks once again about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
We are going to Jerusalem, he said. The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death.
Then they will hand him over to people who are not Jews. People will mock him and beat him. They will crucify him on a cross. On the third day, he will rise from the dead!" A mother asks Jesus for a favor
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt down and asked him for a favor.
What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me," she said, "that one of my sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. And promise me that the other will sit at your left."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink from the cup that I am about to drink from?" They said to him, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "You will certainly drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right or left hand. Those places are for those for whom my Father in heaven has prepared them."
The other ten pupils heard about this. They were angry at the two brothers.
Jesus called his disciples together. He said, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles; they lord it over their people, and their great officials exercise authority over them."
Do not be like this. Rather, whoever wants to be great among you must be your servant.
And everyone who wants to be first must be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give His life as a ransom for many. Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
Two blind men were sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
The crowd told them to be quiet. They asked them to calm down. But the two men shouted even louder, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
Jesus stood up and called out to them, saying, "What do you want me to do for you?"
They said, "Lord, we want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep compassion for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they could see. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
When they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two of his disciples.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. When you enter it, you will find a donkey tied up, and with it its foal. Untie them and bring them to me."
If someone tells you something, say that the Lord needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled. It says,
Say to the daughter of Zion, 'Behold, your king is coming to you, gentle, and riding on a donkey, and on a colt, the foal of a donkey.' (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
Bring a donkey and a colt. They placed their coats on them. Then Jesus sat on the coats.
A very large crowd spread out, placing their coats on the road. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and some people followed him. They all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the entire city was shaken. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the Prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple area. He began driving out all those who were selling and buying there. He overturned the tables of the moneychangers. He also overturned the benches of those who were selling doves.
He said to them: "It is written: 'The Lord said: "My house shall be called a house of prayer" (Isaiah 56:7)], but you are making it into 'a den of robbers' (Jeremiah 7:11)."
Blind and disabled people came to Jesus at the Temple. There, he healed them.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They also saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" When they saw this, they were indignant.
Do you hear what these children are saying? ' asked. 'Yes, ' said Jesus, 'have you not read it in the Scriptures: "I have made you a little lower than the heavenly beings. I have put glory on you and have made you rule over all things." ' (Psalm 8:5-6)
Then Jesus left the people and went out of the city to Bethany. He spent the night there. The fig tree withered.
Early in the morning, Jesus was going back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He came up to it but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "May no fruit ever come from you again!" Immediately the fig tree withered.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. They said, "How did the fig tree dry up so quickly?"
Jesus said, "The words I am about to tell you are the truth. You must believe and not doubt. Then you will be able to do what was done to the fig tree. And you will be able to say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' And it will be done."
If you believe, you will receive what you ask for when you pray." Jesus' authority is being questioned
Jesus entered the temple court. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus said, "I will also ask you one question more. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did the baptism of John come? Was it from heaven, or from men?" And they reasoned with one another, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say to us, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we said, "of the men"? We fear people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
Jesus answered, "I don't know." Jesus said, "Nor will I tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons
What do you think about this? A man had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
The son said, "I will not." But later he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son answered him, "I will, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did what his father wanted? "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "The very thing I tell you is true: tax collectors and prostitutes will enter the Kingdom of God ahead of you."
John came to show you the right way to live. And you did not believe him. But the tax collectors and prostitutes believed him. You saw this. And yet you did not renounce your sins and believe in him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned a piece of land and planted a vineyard. He put a wall around it. He dug a well to press wine in. He also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to some farmers. Then he traveled on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect his share of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw stones at him to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had the first time. The tenants treated them the same way.
Finally, he sent his son to them. He said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will get all the owner's property someday. Come, let's kill him. Then all things will be ours."
So they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When the landowner returns, what will he do with the tenants?
"These bad men will be destroyed," they answered. "Then the vineyard will be rented to other tenants. They will give him his share of the crop at harvest."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what the Scriptures say, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone. This was from the Lord, and it is marvellous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)"
So here is what I say to you: The kingdom of God will be taken from you, and given to people who will produce its fruit.
Everyone who falls on that stone will be broken into pieces. But when the stone falls on anyone, it will crush him.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They knew he was speaking about them.
They searched for a way to arrest him. But they were afraid of the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said,
Here is what the kingdom of heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding banquet for his son.
He sent his servants to those who had been invited to the dinner. The servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent other servants. He said to them, 'Tell those who are invited, "I have prepared my dinner. I have killed my oxen and my finest fattened cattle. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding feast."'
But the people paid no attention. One went to his field. And another went to his work.
The rest seized his servants, mistreated them, and then killed them.
The king became very angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed these murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But those I invited were not worthy to come."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you can find.
The servants went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, whether they were good or bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. There he noticed a man who was not dressed in wedding clothes.
My friend, he asked, how did you get in here without the wedding clothes? The man could not think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Bind his hands and feet. Throw him outside into the darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The plotters went out and made plans to trap Jesus with his own words.
They sent their disciples to him. And they sent along with them the Herodians. They said, "Teacher, we know that you are a man of integrity. You teach the way of God in truth. You do not permit others to dictate to you or to tell you what to do or say. You are not concerned about their importance."
So then tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil plans. He said, "You hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes." They brought him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Whose is this picture? And whose are these words?"
They said, "To Caesar." Then he said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise from the tombs.
On that day, the scribes and Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name."
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second brother, the third brother, and so on, until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Now, when the dead arise from their tombs, whose wife will she be? She was married to everyone, the seven.
Jesus said, "You are going astray, because you do not know the Scriptures nor the power of God."
When the dead rise from their graves, they will not marry. Nor will their fathers give them away in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead being raised? Have you not read what God has told you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. And I am the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead, but of the living.
When the audience heard this, they were amazed at what he had taught them. The most important commandment.
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them, an expert in the law, tested Jesus with a question.
"Master," he asked, "what is the greatest commandment in the law?"
Jesus answered, " 'Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.' " (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: 'Love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
All that was written in the Law and the Prophets depends on these two commandments." "Whose son is the Christ?"
The Pharisees gathered together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he? "The son of David," they answered.
«Why then does David call him 'Lord'? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,»
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him "Lord," how can he be David's son?
No one could answer him with a single word. From that day on, no one dared to ask him any questions afterwards.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The teachers of the law and the Pharisees sit in the seat of Moses,"
Therefore, you must obey them. Do everything they command you to do. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They tie heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of others. But they themselves do not wish to lift a finger to move them.
All that they do is done to be seen by others. They put on their foreheads and arms small boxes containing verses from the Holy Book. They make the boxes very wide. And they lengthen the tags on their coats very long.
They like to sit in the place of honor during dinner. They also like to have the most important seats in the synagogue.
They like to be welcomed into the markets. They like it when people call them 'Rabbi'.
But you should not call him "my Lord." You have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth 'father'. You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called "Teacher." You have one Teacher, the Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
He who exalts himself shall be abased, and he who abases himself shall be exalted.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You shut the kingdom of heaven in men's faces. You yourselves do not enter, nor will you let those enter who are trying.
<.>
You snakes! You offspring of vipers! How will you escape the sentence of hell? Therefore, I send you prophets, wise men, and scribes. Some you will kill and crucify, and some you will flog in your synagogues and persecute from city to city. For truly, I say to you, until heaven and earth pass away, not one jot or one tittle will by any means pass away from the law until all is accomplished. Whoever then relaxes one of the least of these commandments and teaches others to do the same, will be called the least in the kingdom of heaven. For I tell you that unless your righteousness surpasses that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven.
O ye guides, ye blind! Ye say: Whosoever shall swear by the temple, it is nothing; but whosoever shall swear by the gold of the temple, he must be bound to pay.
You are blind and foolish! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple which makes the gold sacred?
She also says: "If someone swears by the name of the altar, it is of no consequence. But whoever swears by the name of the gift that is upon him, he must fulfill the oath."
O blind men! Which is more important? Is it the gift? Or is it the altar, which sanctifies the gift?
For he that heareth say, Thou shalt do no murder, and doeth any of them, shall be cut off from his people.
And whoever swears by the name of the temple, swears by his name and by him who dwells in him.
And everyone who swears by the name of heaven thereby swears by the name of God's throne and by the one who sits upon it.
You hypocrites, well-stocked with the teachings of the Pharisees and experts in the law! You are like tombs, beautiful on the outside but filled with death and decay inside. You travel over land and sea to convert one person to your religion, and then you celebrate their conversion as if you have achieved a great victory. You tie up heavy bundles of rules and laws and place them on the shoulders of others, but you yourselves do not lift a finger to help them carry their burden. You build tombs for the prophets and decorate them with beautiful inscriptions, but it was your ancestors who killed those very prophets. You appear to people as wise and humble, but inwardly you are full of hypocrisy and pride. The words I have spoken to you are not my own; they are the words of the Father who sent me to speak them to you. If you do not believe that I am the one I claim to be, at least believe what I have told you about myself. For I have not spoken on my own authority; rather, the Father who dwells in me has spoken these words to you. And you will receive power to become my witnesses in Jerusalem, in all Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.
O blind guides! You strain out the smallest gnat from your drink. But you swallow a whole camel!
What terrible things you are, you teachers of the law and you Pharisees, you impostors! You clean the outside of the cup and the dish, but inside you are full of greed and wickedness, you hypocrites! Blind guides! You strain out a gnat but swallow a camel.
You blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and the plate, then the outside will also be clean.
Oh, what terrible people you are, you teachers of the law and you Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You are like tombs that are whitewashed. They look beautiful on the outside, but inside they are full of the bones of the dead. And they are also full of other unclean and corrupt things.
The same is true for you. From the outside, you look like you are doing what is right. But on the inside, you are filled with what is wrong. You pretend that you are not what you are.
Oh, what horror, you teachers of the law and you Pharisees! You impostors! You build tombs for the prophets and decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And she says, "If we were living in the days of those who preceded us, we would not have done what they did. We would not have helped in the killing of the prophets."
You bear witness against yourselves. You admit that you are the children of those who killed the prophets.
Complete the cycle of sins that those before you began!
Your nest is made of poisonous vipers! How will you escape being sentenced to the fire?
Therefore, I send you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, and you will crucify some of them on the cross, and you will flog others in your synagogues. You will pursue them from city to city.
Therefore you will pay the blood of all the righteous people that has been shed upon the earth. I mean from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah the son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am about to tell you is true. All of this will happen to those who are now alive.
Oh Jerusalem! Oh Jerusalem! You are killing the prophets and throwing stones to kill those who are sent to you. How often I have desired to gather your people together, like a hen gathers her brood under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house has become empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
End Times Signs
Jesus left the temple. He was walking away when his disciples approached him. They wanted to draw his attention to the temple buildings.
Do you see all these things? Jesus asked. What I tell you is true. Not one stone will be left here on another. Every stone will be thrown down.
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, His disciples came to Him privately. They said to Him, "Tell us, when will these things happen? And what will be the sign of Your coming? And what will be the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus said, "Watch out! Be on guard lest someone rob you by means of a lie."
Many will come in my name. They will claim, "I am the Messiah!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear about wars. You will also hear people talking about wars of the future. Do not worry. Those things must happen. But the end is not yet come.
Nation will fight against nation. Kingdom will fight against kingdom. People will be hungry. There will be earthquakes in many places.
All these are the beginning of the birth pangs.
Then people will hand you over to be mistreated and killed. All nations will hate you because of me.
In those days, many will depart from their faith. They will hate each other. They will betray each other to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever endures to the end will be saved.
This good news about the kingdom will be proclaimed throughout the world for all nations to hear. It will be a testimony. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that destroys." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) In time you will see it standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Those present in Judea must flee to the mountains.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to get something.
No one in the field should go back to get their coat.
How terrible it would be in those days for pregnant women! How horrible for nursing mothers!
Pray that you don't have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than anything else that has happened in the world from the beginning of time until now. And there will never be anything like it again.
If time was not shortened, no one would survive. But because of God's chosen people, it will be shortened.
At that time, someone might tell you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or "There he is!" Do not believe it.
The Antichrist and false prophets will appear. They will perform great wonders and signs. They will try to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
Look, I already told you.
If someone tells you, "He is far in the desert," do not go there. Or if someone says, "He is deep in the house," do not believe it.
The lightning that comes from the east can be seen in the west. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever there is a carcass.
After the terrible suffering of those days, "the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light; the stars will fall from the sky, and the heavens will be shaken." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with great power and glory.
He will send His angels with a loud trumpet call. He will gather His chosen people from all four corners. He will bring them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. Once its branches become tender and its leaves appear, you know that summer is near.
In the same way, when you see all those things happening, you know that the end is near. It is at the door.
What I am about to tell you is true. The people who were living at that time would certainly not die until all those things happened.
The heaven and the earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away. No man knows the day or the hour.
No one knows about that day or hour. Even the angels in heaven don't know. The Son doesn't know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They did all those things until the day Noah entered the ark.
They had no idea what was going to happen until the flood came and swept them all away. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Two men will be in the field; one will be taken and the other left.
Two women will grind using a manual mill. One will be taken and the other will be left.
Therefore, fear. You do not know when your Lord will come.
You must understand something. Suppose the homeowner knew exactly what time of night the thief would come. Then he would remain vigilant. He would not give in to the breaking of his home.
Therefore you must also be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
Assume a master entrusts one of his slaves with the management of the other slaves in his household. The slave's task is to provide their food at the appropriate time. The master wants a faithful and wise slave for this matter.
It will be better for the servant if the master finds him doing his work when the master returns.
What I am about to tell you is true. That master will make that slave responsible for everything he owns.
But suppose that slave is wicked; suppose he says to himself, "My master is often absent for a long time."
Suppose he begins to strike the other servants. And suppose he eats and drinks with the heavy drinkers.
The master of that slave will return on a day the slave does not expect. He will return in an hour the slave does not know.
Then the Master will cut him into pieces. He will send him to the place where the protesters go. There, the people will weep and grind their teeth.
The Story of the Ten Bride Girls
Here is how the Kingdom of Heaven will be at that time. Ten bridesmaids took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were fools. And five were wise.
The fools took their lamps, but they didn't take any olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in jars with their lamps.
The groom did not come for a long time. All the bridesmaids got tired and fell asleep.
At midnight, someone shouted, "Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!"
Then all the matrons woke up and prepared their lamps.
The ignorant said to the wise, "Give us of your oil, for our lamps are going out."
"No," they replied. "There may not be enough for everyone. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves."
So they went to buy the oil. But on their way, the bridegroom arrived. The girls who were ready went in with him to the wedding supper. Then the door was shut.
Later, the rest of the incubators came too. "Sir! Sir!" they said. "Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Beware. You do not know on what day or hour the bridegroom will come. The Tale of the Three Servants
Again, here is what the Kingdom of Heaven will be like. A man was going on a journey. He called his servants and entrusted his property to them.
He gave one ten thousand dollars. He gave another four thousand dollars. He gave the third two thousand dollars. The man gave each servant the amount he knew the servant could take care of. Then he set out on his journey.
The server who received the ten thousand dollars immediately went and put his money to work. He earned another ten thousand dollars.
The one who won 4,000 dollars won an additional 4,000 dollars.
But the man who received 2000 dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of these servants returned. He wanted to gather all the money that they had earned.
The man who received 10,000 dollars brought the other 10,000 dollars. He said, "Sir, you trusted me with 10,000 dollars. Look, I have earned another 10,000 dollars."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things, I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
The man who also had $4,000 came. "Sir," he said, "you trusted me with $4,000. Look, I have won an additional $4,000."
His master answered, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things, I will make you ruler over many things. Come and share your master's joy!"
Then the man to whom the $2,000 had been given came. He said, "Sir, I know you are a strict man. You gather where you did not sow, and you gather where you did not scatter seed."
So you were scared. You went out and buried your $2000 in the ground. See, this is what's yours.
His master answered and said unto him, O wicked and slothful servant! Thou knewest that I reap where I sowed not; and gather where I have not strawed:
Well, you should have deposited my money in the bank. When I returned, I would have received it with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants, "Take his ten thousand dollars away from him. Give it to the one who has one hundred thousand dollars."
Everyone who has will be given more. They will have more than enough. And what about those who have nothing? Even what little they have will be taken away from them.
Cast out that worthless slave into the outer darkness, there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.' 'Sheep and Goats'
Then the Son of Man will come in his glory. And all the angels will come with him. Then he will sit on his glorious throne. And the people of all nations will be brought before him.
All nations will be gathered before him. He will separate people into two groups. He will be like a shepherd separating the sheep from the goats.
He will place the sheep on his right and the goats on his left.
Then the king speaks to those on his right, saying, "My Father has blessed you; come, take what is yours, for the kingdom has been prepared for you since the creation of the world."
I was hungry. And you gave me something to eat. I was thirsty. And you gave me something to drink. I was a stranger. And you invited me in.
I needed clothes. And you gave them to me. I was sick. And you took care of me. I was in prison. And you came to visit me.
Then those who had done the right thing will answer them: "Lord," they will ask him, "when did we see you hungry and feed you? When did we see you thirsty and give you a drink?"
When did we see you a stranger and invite you in? When did we see you needing clothes and give you some?
When did we see you sick or in prison and visit you?
The King will reply: "What I am about to tell you is the truth. Whatever you did to one of these least brothers of mine, you did it to me."
Then He will say to those on His left, 'Cursed ones! Go away from Me into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels.'
I was hungry. But you didn't give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you didn't give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger. But you did not let me in. I needed clothes. But you did not give me any. I was sick and in prison. But you did not care for me.
They will also answer: 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not help you? When did we see you as a stranger or needing clothes or sick or in prison and did not help you?'
He will answer: "What I tell you is true. Anything you did not do for one of these least ones, you did not do for me."
Then they will go to be punished forever. As for those who did what is right, they will receive eternal life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
Jesus finished saying all these things. Then he said to his disciples,
You know, Easter is in two days. The Son of Man will be handed over to be crucified on a cross.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. They met in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They made plans to arrest Jesus cunningly. They wanted to kill him.
But not during the feast, they said. It might stir up the people. A woman poured perfume on Jesus
Jesus was in Bethany. He was at the home of a man named Simon, who had been suffering from a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with a special sealed jar containing a very precious perfume. She poured the perfume on his head while he was reclining at the table.
When the students saw this, they became angry. "Why is there such waste?" they asked.
The perfume could have been sold for a high price. The money could have been given to the poor.
Jesus was aware of this. He said to them, "Why do you disturb this woman? She has done a beautiful thing for me."
You will always have the poor with you, but you will not always have me.
She poured perfume on my body to prepare me for burial.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. What I have done will be told everywhere the good news is preached throughout the world. It will be told as a memorial to her." Judas agrees to betray Jesus
One of the twelve went to the chief priests. His name was Judas Iscariot.
He asked, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" So they counted out for him thirty pieces of silver.
Since then, Judas watched for an opportune time to deliver Jesus to them. The Lord's Supper
It was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus, saying, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
He answered, "Go into the city to a certain man, and say to him, 'The Teacher says, My time is near, and I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples.'"
So the disciples did what Jesus had commanded them to do. They brought the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was sitting at the table with the twelve.
While they were eating, he said, "What I am about to tell you is true. One of you will betray me to my enemies."
The disciples became very sad. One by one, they began to say to him, "Not I, Lord, surely not I?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand in the bowl with me is the one who will betray me."
The Son of Man will go away just as it is written about him. But how terrible it will be for the one who hands over the Son of Man! It would be better for him if he had never been born.
It was Judas who would betray him. He said, "Surely it is not I, Lord?" Jesus answered, "You have said so."
While they were eating, Jesus took bread. He thanked and broke it. He gave it to His disciples and said: "Take this and eat, this is My body."
Then he took the cup. He thanked God and gave it to them. He said, "All of you drink from it."
This is my blood of the new covenant. It is poured out to forgive the sins of many.
Here is what I say to you. From now on, I will never drink wine with you again until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them, "This very night before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times because of me. For it is written, 'The Lord said to me, "I will break off the shepherd staff that remains over the earth." ' " (Zechariah 13:7)
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee.'
Peter replied, "Others may pull back because of you, but I will never do so."
"What I am about to tell you is the truth," Jesus answered. "It will happen tonight. Before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times."
But Peter said, "I will have to die with you. I will never say that I don't know you." All the other disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in the Olive Garden
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemani. He said to them, "Sit here, and I will go over there to pray."
Peter took John and James, the sons of Zebedee, along with him. He began to feel sorrow and deep distress.
Then he said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful. I feel that death is near. Stay here. Watch with me."
He went a little way forward; then fell, face downward. He prayed, "My Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me; nevertheless, not my will, but thine be done."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Are you not able to watch with me for one hour?"
Watch and pray. Then you will not fall into temptation when you are tested. The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.
Jesus went out again. He prayed, saying, "Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not as I will, but as You will."
Then he came back. Once again he found them sleeping. They could not keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went away once more. And for the third time he prayed the same thing.
Then he went back to the disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and taking your rest? Look! The hour is already near. The Son of Man is about to be betrayed into the hands of sinners."
Arise! Let us go! Here is he that betrayeth me unto them! "Jesus is arrested"
While Jesus was still speaking, Judas arrived. He was one of the twelve. A large crowd was with him, carrying swords and clubs. The chief priests, the captains of the people, and the elders sent them.
Judah, who was going to betray Jesus, had arranged a sign with them. He said, "The one I kiss is the man." "Arrest him."
So Judas went to Jesus at once. He said to him, "Greetings, Teacher!" and kissed him.
Jesus answered, "My friend, do what you have come for." Then the men stepped forward. They seized Jesus and arrested him.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword. He struck the servant of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back into its place, for all those who take the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how will the holy books be fulfilled then? They say it must happen this way.
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowd. He said, "Am I leading a band of armed men against you? Have you come out with swords and sticks to arrest me? For I sat daily in the temple teaching, and you did not arrest me."
"But all this has taken place to fulfill the words of the prophets." Then all the disciples left him and fled. Jesus is taken to the high priest
Those who had arrested Jesus brought him to Caiaphas, the high priest. The teachers of the law and the elders had assembled there.
Not too far away, Peter followed Jesus. He went straight to the chief priest's courtyard. He entered and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
The high priests and the entire Council of the Sanhedrin were searching for something to use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they did not find any evidence, although many false witnesses came forward. Finally, two more witnesses came forward.
They said, "This man claims, 'I am able to destroy the temple of God. I can rebuild it in three days.' "
Then the high priest stood up. He asked Jesus, "Will you not answer? What are these men charging against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. The high priest said to him, "I adjure you by the living God to tell us, are you the Christ, the Son of God?"
"Yes. It is as you say," Jesus answered. "But this is what I tell all of you. In the coming days, you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the Mighty One. You will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his clothes. "He has spoken a very evil thing against God!" he said. "Why do we need any more witnesses? You have heard him say this evil thing."
What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
Then they spat in his face. They hit him with their hands. Others slapped his face.
They said, "Tell us, O Christ! Who struck you?" Peter says he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A female servant came to him and said, "You also were with Jesus of Galilee."
But in front of everyone, Peter said that he was not. "I don't know what you are talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate that leads to the courtyard. There another woman saw him. She said to the people, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
Again he said it was not so. With an oath he protested, "I do not know the man!"
After a short while, those standing there approached Peter. "You must be one of them," they said. "The way you talk gives you away."
Then Peter began to curse himself. He swore to them and said, "I do not know the man!" Immediately a cock crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said. "A cock will not crow," Jesus had told him. "before it does, you will have said three times that you do not know me." Peter went outside. He broke down and wept bitterly.
Judas hangs himself.
It was early in the morning. All the chief priests and the elders of the people decided to kill Jesus.
They bound him and led him away. Then they handed him over to Pilate, who was the governor.
Judas, who had betrayed him, saw that Jesus had been condemned to death. He felt deep remorse and sorrow for what he had done. So he took back the thirty pieces of silver to the priests and elders.
"I have made a mistake," he said. "I handed over an innocent man." They replied, "What does it matter to us?" "This is your problem."
Judah threw the silver into the temple and went out. Then he went and hanged himself.
The leading priests picked up the coins. "We cannot put this blood money into the Temple treasury," they declared. "It is tainted money. It was paid for the murder of a man."
Therefore, they decided to use the money to buy the Potter's Field. People from other countries will be buried there.
This is why it is still called Blood Field to this day.
Then the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which the people of Israel valued him."
They weighed out the money for the potter's field, as the Lord instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12,13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) Jesus is brought before Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" "Yes. It is as you say," Jesus answered.
But when the leading priests and the elders brought accusations against him, he made no reply.
Then Pilate asked, "Don't you hear the charges against you?"
But Jesus answered not a word to any of the charges. The governor was exceedingly astonished.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people would choose whom they wanted.
At that time they had a notorious prisoner called Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate asked them, "Which one do you want me to release for you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Christ?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were jealous. And he knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message. It said, "Have nothing to do with that man. He is innocent. I have suffered greatly in a dream today because of him."
But the priests, the chief priests and the elders stirred up the crowd to have Barabbas requested and Jesus killed.
The governor said, "Which one do you want me to release?" And they said, "Barabbas."
"What shall I do then with Jesus called Christ?" Pilate asked. "Everyone, 'Crucify him!'" they all answered.
"Why? What has he done wrong?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was accomplishing nothing. Instead, the crowd began to grow angry. He took water and washed his hands before them. "I am not responsible for the death of this man," he said. "You are the ones responsible for it!"
All the people answered, "We and our children will bear the guilt of his death!"
Barabbas was released by Pilate. However, he ordered Jesus to be flogged. Then he handed him over to be crucified on the cross. Soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. The rest of the soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him and put a purple cloak on him.
Then they twisted the thorns together to make a crown. They put it on his head. They put a staff in his right hand. Then they fell down on their knees in front of him and mocked him. "We honor you, O King of the Jews!" they said.
They urinated on him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After mocking him, they took off the robe. They put back on him his own clothes. Then they led him away to be crucified on the cross. Jesus is crucified on the cross.
On their way out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene. His name was Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They arrived at a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
There they mixed the wine with bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. After tasting it, He refused to drink it.
When they crucified him, they divided his clothes by casting lots.
They sat there and watched him.
Above his head they placed the written accusation against him. It read: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
Two robbers were crucified with him, one on his right and one on his left.
Those who passed by scoffed at Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads
And he said, "Will you destroy the sanctuary and rebuild it in three days? Then save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the son of God!"
In the same way, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders mocked him.
They said, "He saved others, but he cannot save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross, and then we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if he wants to. He is the one who said: "I am the son of God."
In the same way, the thieves who were being crucified with Jesus also mocked him. Jesus dies
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?" which means, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those standing there heard Jesus cry out. "He is calling Elijah," they said.
Immediately someone ran and got a sponge. He filled it with sour wine, put it on a reed, and offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let us see whether Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the veil of the temple was torn from top to bottom. The earth shook and the rocks were split.
Tombs were opened. The lives of many holy people who had died were restored to life.
They came out of the tombs. After Jesus rose from the dead, they entered the holy city. There they appeared to many people.
The Roman centurion and those who were guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened. They were terrified. They exclaimed, "Truly, he was the Son of God!"
Not too far away, many women were watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was one of them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was also there. So was the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus is buried
As evening approached, a wealthy man from the town of Ramah came. His name was Joseph. He became a disciple of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate commanded that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth.
He placed him in his new grave which he had dug in the rock. Then he threw a large stone in front of the entrance to the grave. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards of the tomb
The next day was the day after the preparation. The chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
Master, they said: "We remember something the liar said while he was still alive. He claimed: 'After three days I will rise again.'"
So he ordered the tomb to be made secure until the third day; otherwise, his disciples might come and steal the body away. Then they would go and tell the people that Jesus had risen from the dead. And this last lie would be worse than the first.
"Take some guards with you," Pilate replied. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as possible."
So they went and made the tomb secure. They placed a seal on the stone and put some guards on duty.
Jesus rises from the dead.
Now the Sabbath is past. It is the first day of the week, the dawn. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to see the tomb.
A strong earthquake occurred. An angel descended from the Lord from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He rolled back the stone and sat on it.
His body was glowing like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid, I know that you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen from the dead, just as he said he would! Come and see the place where he lay.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He will go before you to Galilee. There you will see him." Now I have told you.
The women went out and fled from the tomb. They were afraid, but filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Suddenly they met Jesus. He said, "Peace be with you!" They came to him and touched his feet and worshiped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards reported to the chief priests.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the principal priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave the soldiers a large sum of money.
They told the soldiers, "We want you to say, 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we will settle his debt. That will save you from trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has been spread among the Jews to this day. Jesus' final instructions to his disciples.
Then the eleven disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw him, they worshipped him. But some still have their doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Therefore, you must go and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And be assured that I am with you always, to the end of the age.
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the good news about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "I send my messenger to you, who prepares the way." (Kings 3:1)
A messenger calls in the wilderness: "Prepare the way for the Lord, make his paths straight." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also preached that people should be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people from the countryside of Judea went out to him. And all the people from Jerusalem went out also. And they confessed their sins, and he baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. And he ate locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John used to preach: "After me will come one who is mightier than I am. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of his sandals."
I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. And John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. At that moment, he saw the sky being torn open and the Spirit descending on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven spoke to him. It said, "You are my son, and I love you. I am very pleased with you."
Immediately, the Spirit sent Jesus out into the wilderness.
He was in the desert for forty days. The devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm him. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went to Galilee. He was preaching the good news of God.
The time has come, he said. The Kingdom of God is near. Repent of your sins and believe in the Good News!
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Come, follow Me, said Jesus. I will make you fishers of men.
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little forward. And as he did so, he saw James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately he called them. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men. Then they followed Jesus. Jesus Drives Out an Evil Spirit
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching. He taught them with authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
At that moment, a man cried out in their temple. He was possessed by an evil spirit. He said,
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
"Be quiet!" said Jesus sternly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a scream.
Everyone was amazed and they asked each other, "What is this? A new teaching, with authority! He even commands evil spirits and they obey him."
News of Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those who were with him left the synagogue. Immediately they went with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her. He took her hand and helped her to stand. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They had all sorts of diseases. He also drove out many demons. But he did not let the demons speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning and still dark. Jesus got up from his sleep and went out of the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they called out, "Everyone has been looking for you!"
Jesus said, "Let's go to another place. I want to go to the neighboring towns. I must preach there too. That is why I came."
Jesus traveled throughout all the region of Galilee. And he taught in their synagogues. And he cast out demons also. Jesus heals a man who was suffering from a skin disease.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt before him and begged him, saying, "If you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus was filled with profound concern. He reached out and touched the man. He said, "I am ready for that." "Cleanse!"
Immediately the disease left him. He was cured.
Jesus sent him away immediately. He gave the man a strong warning.
Don't tell anyone about this, he said. Go and present yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. It will be a testimony to the priest and the people that you are "clean."
But the man went out and began to talk at once. He spread the news to everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter the city openly. He stayed outside in deserted places. Nevertheless, people came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned to his house.
He gathered so many people that there was no more room, not even outside the door. And Jesus began to teach them the word.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get him close to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made a hole in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man through it on a mat.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the teachers of the law were sitting there, reasoning within themselves,
Why is this man talking like this? He is saying something very wicked indeed! Only God can forgive sins!
Immediately Jesus knew what they were thinking in their hearts. He said to them, "Why are you thinking these things?"
Is it easier to tell this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Stand up, take your mat and walk"?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
"I tell you," he said, "get up. Take your mat and go home."
The man got up, took his mat, and walked away. All the people were amazed and praised God, saying, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Again Jesus went out beside the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd came to him, and he began to teach them.
As he walked, he saw Levi, the son of Alphaeus, sitting at the tax office. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And Levi rose and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at the home of Lazarus. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that followed Jesus.
There were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard that. And he said to them: "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I was not sent to call the righteous, but sinners, so that they may come to me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees used to fast. Some people came to Jesus and said, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also fast. But your disciples do not fast. Why is this?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as the bridegroom is with them."
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; and on that day they will go out into the fields and will not eat.
People do not sew a piece of new cloth onto old clothing. If they did this, the new piece would tear away from the old. This would make the tear even worse.
People don't pour new wine into old jars. If they do that, the wine will break the jars. Then both the wine and the jars are ruined. No, everyone pours new wine into new jars." Jesus Lord of the Sabbath
On a Saturday, Jesus was walking with his disciples through fields of grain. The disciples began to pick some heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, it is not lawful to do this on the Sabbath. Why are your disciples doing this?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate some of the showbread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it. David also gave some of it to his men.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath."
For the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath Day.
Healing on Saturday
At another time Jesus entered the synagogue. There was a man whose hand was withered and crooked.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man with a weak and crooked hand. He said, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the law say? What should we do on the Sabbath? Is it right to do good or to do evil? Should we save life or destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them with anger. He was very disturbed because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and his hand was restored like the hand of a normal person.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. Great crowds followed him from Galilee.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and from the region of Ammon. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus instructed his disciples to prepare a small boat for him, to prevent people from pressing around him.
Jesus had healed many people. Therefore, those who were sick would come forward to touch him.
When the people saw him, spirits impure fell before him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up on the mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
He appointed twelve of them and called them apostles. From then on, they were with him. He also sent them out to preach.
They had the power to cast out demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus. And Judas the son of James, and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He was the one who would later deliver Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and swallowing the gnat.
Jesus entered a house. Once again a large crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about it. So they went to take care of him. They said, "He is out of his mind."
There were some Pharisees. They came down from Jerusalem. They said, "He is controlled by Beelzebub! He casts out demons by the power of the prince of demons."
Jesus called them and spoke to them in parables, saying, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights itself, it cannot stand.
If a family divides, it cannot stand.
